Argument Against Measure K
There is only one reason Measure K is on the ballot. It is a power grab that
allows the current City Council to reset and extend their terms in office,
plus stay longer then currently allowed. Measure K’s title should be “Term
Limit Extension Measure”.
82% of Orange voters approved the current city council term limits.
Measure K not only overturns what voters overwhelmingly put into
place, it disguises a 50% increase in City Council Member terms as a
“local control” measure, allows sitting council members and the mayor
to effectively start over with a free pass on years already served, and
stay an additional 12 years. If approved, we could see outcomes where
incumbents remain in power until 2040.
Claims that moving Orange to a charter city will restore local control are
just not true. Moving to a charter city structure with a rushed and false
local control narrative can come at the expense of citizen oversight and
accountability. This can lead to abuses of power and decision-making that
prioritizes special interests over those of the community.
Becoming a charter city will not eliminate the requirement to uphold
state housing requirements.
Becoming a charter city will make it easier for the city council to impose
local taxes.
Becoming a charter city historically increases city legal costs, which could
result in higher taxes and reduced public services.
Measure K was placed on the ballot by a tight 4-3 vote of the city council
and was written without input of a dedicated, independent citizens’
charter commission. Having the independent oversight of a citizens’
commission would have at least ensured the rules were written by
residents of Orange, free from any conflicts of interest.
Vote No on extending term limits for the current city council.
